# CLAUDE.md — __PROJECT_NAME__

Instructions for AI agents and humans working in this repository. They
are **rules, not suggestions**: this service is built on
[`tempest-fastapi-sdk`](https://mauriciobenjamin700.github.io/tempest-fastapi-sdk/),
and the whole point of the SDK is that every Tempest service has the same
shape. Code that ignores the shape costs the next person a rewrite.

Pinned SDK requirement: `__SDK_DEP__`
Extras enabled in this project: `__SDK_EXTRAS__`

## Before you write anything

1. **Check the SDK first.** Most of what a service needs already exists
   there — repositories, pagination, auth, exceptions, cache, queues,
   uploads, admin, HTML pages. Reimplementing it is the single most
   common defect in a scaffolded project. The table under
   [Do not reimplement](#do-not-reimplement-these) is the shortlist; the
   [full docs](https://mauriciobenjamin700.github.io/tempest-fastapi-sdk/)
   are the authority.
2. **Follow the layer that owns the change.** Adding a query? That is a
   repository. Adding a rule? A service. Adding an endpoint? A router.
   Never two at once.
3. **Type everything and docstring everything.** No exceptions, not even
   for a two-line helper.

## Layers

```text
src/
├── api/routers/            # one router per module; /api set once in routers/api.py
├── api/dependencies/       # request providers (get_<x>_controller) + infra singletons
├── controllers/<context>/  # orchestration across services. No DB access.
├── services/<context>/     # business rules. Calls repositories.
├── db/repositories/<context>/  # SQLAlchemy async queries. No business rules.
├── db/models/<context>/    # ORM models
├── schemas/                # Pydantic DTOs (request/response)
├── scripts/                # composition root (build_*) + work tasks run
├── core/                   # settings, enums, types, exceptions
├── tasks/ (with [tasks])   # task registration, names, dispatcher, worker
└── ui/ (with [ssr])        # pages, layout, components, styles — HTML only
```

The dependency direction is one-way, and it is the whole contract:

| Layer | May import | Never imports |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `api/routers` | `controllers`, `schemas`, `ui` | `db`, `services` directly |
| `api/dependencies` | `scripts`, `controllers`, `core` | `db/models` |
| `ui` | `schemas`, other parts of `ui` | `controllers`, `services`, `db` |
| `controllers` | `services`, `schemas` | `db`, `ui` |
| `services` | `db/repositories`, `schemas` | HTTP types, `ui`, other services |
| `db/repositories` | `db/models`, `schemas` | other repositories, business rules, HTTP |

**A router that opens a session, or a service that builds an
`HTTPException`, is a defect** — fix the layering, do not add a
workaround.

### Context packages

Every module of `controllers/`, `services/`, `db/repositories/` and
`db/models/` lives in a **context subpackage** and holds **one class**:
`services/products/product.py` defines `ProductService` and nothing else.
Contexts this project starts with:

- `system` — what the service says about itself (`ServiceInfoController`).
@@if auth@@
- `users` — accounts and their access (`UserModel`).
@@endif@@

A new domain is a new context: a plural noun (`products`, `orders`,
`billing`), created in each layer it touches. Any name is accepted; the
list above is where to start, not a limit.

Every context package re-exports its classes **relatively**, and so does
the layer package above it:

```python
# src/services/products/__init__.py
from .product import ProductService as ProductService

__all__: list[str] = ["ProductService"]
```

Outside an `__init__.py`, import from the **layer package**
(`from src.services import ProductService`), never from the module the
class lives in.

### Composition root

`src/scripts/composition.py` is the one place the object graph is
assembled: one `build_*` function per entry point, taking only what the
caller holds (a session, the settings). The request providers in
`src/api/dependencies/controllers.py` call them with what `Depends`
resolved; a task, a queue subscriber, a seed script and the test suite
call the same functions. **No class assembles itself** — there is no
`from_session`.

## Adding a feature: the exact order

Every new domain follows these steps, in this order. Skipping one
produces a service that looks unlike every other Tempest service.

### 1. Schema (`src/schemas/`)

Every field carries `title`, `description` and `examples` — they are what
the OpenAPI document shows the client. A field holding another schema needs
only `title` and `description`. `tests/test_schemas.py` enforces it with
`assert_schema_metadata(src.schemas)`, and `tempest lint-schemas` lists
what is missing:

```python
# src/schemas/product.py
from pydantic import Field
from tempest_fastapi_sdk import BaseResponseSchema, BaseSchema


class ProductCreateSchema(BaseSchema):
    """Payload accepted when creating a product."""

    name: str = Field(
        title="Name",
        description="Name shown to the customer; unique.",
        examples=["Camisa"],
    )
    price_cents: int = Field(
        title="Price",
        description="Price in cents.",
        examples=[4990],
    )


class ProductResponseSchema(BaseResponseSchema):
    """Product returned by the API."""

    name: str = Field(
        title="Name",
        description="Name shown to the customer.",
        examples=["Camisa"],
    )
    price_cents: int = Field(
        title="Price",
        description="Price in cents.",
        examples=[4990],
    )
```

A domain enum subclasses `BaseStrEnum` and its name **ends in `Enum`**:

```python
# src/core/enums.py
from tempest_fastapi_sdk import BaseStrEnum


class ProductStatusEnum(BaseStrEnum):
    """Where a product is in its lifecycle."""

    DRAFT = "draft"
    PUBLISHED = "published"
```

The suffix is a rule, not taste: `tempest check` fails on a `BaseStrEnum`
subclass without it (`ENUM001`), naming the class it expects. The
database type is the same either way — `enum_type_name` drops one
trailing `Enum`, so `ProductStatus` and `ProductStatusEnum` are both
`product_status_enum` — which is why the rename never needs a migration.

### 2. Model (`src/db/models/products/`)

Add the table name to `src/db/configs/names.py` first — it is the single
source of truth, and the model reads it from there:

```python
# src/db/configs/names.py
PRODUCT_TABLE_NAME = "products"
```

```python
# src/db/models/products/product.py
from sqlalchemy.orm import Mapped, mapped_column
from tempest_fastapi_sdk import BaseModel

from src.db.configs import PRODUCT_TABLE_NAME


class ProductModel(BaseModel):
    """Products table."""

    __tablename__ = PRODUCT_TABLE_NAME

    name: Mapped[str] = mapped_column(index=True)
    price_cents: Mapped[int] = mapped_column(default=0)
```

```python
# src/db/models/products/__init__.py
from .product import ProductModel as ProductModel

__all__: list[str] = ["ProductModel"]
```

Re-export the model from `src/db/models/__init__.py`
(`from .products import ProductModel as ProductModel`) — Alembic only sees
the models that package imports — and the table name from
`src/db/configs/__init__.py`.

`BaseModel` already provides `id`, `created_at` and `updated_at` — never
redeclare them.

A foreign key spells the same symbol rather than the string again:
`ForeignKey(f"{PRODUCT_TABLE_NAME}.id")`. SQLAlchemy resolves that
target only when it configures the mappers, so a rename that misses one
side fails at startup — or inside a migration pointing at a table that
no longer exists — instead of where it was typed.

Then write the migration — the schema only changes through one:

```bash
tempest db revision -m "add products"
```

The app applies pending revisions on startup (`sync_schema_async` in the
lifespan of `src/api/app.py`), so the next boot creates the table. Never
call `create_all` to get there: it adds no column to a table that already
exists, and the next migration then runs against a schema it does not
expect.

### 3. Repository (`src/db/repositories/products/`)

```python
# src/db/repositories/products/product.py
from sqlalchemy.ext.asyncio import AsyncSession
from tempest_fastapi_sdk import BaseRepository

from src.core.exceptions import ProductNotFoundError
from src.db.models import ProductModel
from src.schemas import ProductResponseSchema


class ProductRepository(BaseRepository[ProductModel]):
    """Data access for products."""

    def __init__(self, session: AsyncSession) -> None:
        """Bind the repository to a session.

        Args:
            session (AsyncSession): The active async session.
        """
        super().__init__(
            session,
            model=ProductModel,
            not_found_exception=ProductNotFoundError,
            not_found_message="Produto não encontrado",
        )

    def map_to_response(self, instance: ProductModel) -> ProductResponseSchema:
        """Map a row to its API response schema.

        Args:
            instance (ProductModel): The ORM row.

        Returns:
            ProductResponseSchema: The response schema.
        """
        return ProductResponseSchema.model_validate(instance)
```

```python
# src/db/repositories/products/__init__.py
from .product import ProductRepository as ProductRepository

__all__: list[str] = ["ProductRepository"]
```

`map_to_response` is the **one method every repository must implement** —
the base class raises `NotImplementedError` otherwise, and the service
layer calls it on every read.

You inherit `add`, `get`, `get_by_id`, `get_or_none`, `first`, `list`,
`paginate`, `cursor_paginate`, `update`, `delete`, `soft_delete`,
`count`, `exists`, `search`, `full_text_search`, the `bulk_*` family and
`transaction` / `savepoint`. **Write a new method only for a query those
cannot express.** A repository never imports another repository: a query
over two tables joins in its own `select()`, and combining two
repositories is a service's job.

### 4. Service (`src/services/products/`)

```python
# src/services/products/product.py
from tempest_fastapi_sdk import BaseService

from src.core.exceptions import ProductNameTakenError
from src.db.repositories import ProductRepository
from src.schemas import ProductCreateSchema, ProductResponseSchema


class ProductService(BaseService[ProductRepository, ProductResponseSchema]):
    """Business rules for products."""

    async def create(self, data: ProductCreateSchema) -> ProductResponseSchema:
        """Create a product, rejecting a duplicate name.

        Args:
            data (ProductCreateSchema): The creation payload.

        Returns:
            ProductResponseSchema: The created product.

        Raises:
            ProductNameTakenError: When the name is already registered.
        """
        if await self.repository.exists({"name": data.name}):
            raise ProductNameTakenError()
        instance = self.repository.map_to_model(data.model_dump())
        instance = await self.repository.add(instance)
        return self.repository.map_to_response(instance)
```

```python
# src/services/products/__init__.py
from .product import ProductService as ProductService

__all__: list[str] = ["ProductService"]
```

`BaseService` already gives you `get_by_id`, `get_or_none`, `list`,
`paginate`, `count`, `exists`, `update` and `delete`, each wired to
`repository.map_to_response`. Override one only when a domain rule
belongs in it; add a new method for a use case the base does not cover.

These are **rules**, not style — each one is a defect in review:

- **Every service subclasses `BaseService[Repository, ResponseSchema]`.**
  The only exception is a service with no repository at all (a static
  catalogue read from a file); it says so with
  `# tempest: no-base-service` on its `class` line.
- **No `__init__` that only stores the repository.** `BaseService`
  already does it.
- **A service receives repositories, never the `AsyncSession` and never
  another service.** Combining two services is the controller's job.
- **One public class per module in `services/`, and no module-level
  functions.** A helper that does not belong to the class (formatting, a
  regex, a pure calculation) goes to `src/utils/`; three services in one
  `feeds.py` are three modules.
- **No repository method that only forwards to the base.** A
  `list_all()` whose body is `return await self.list()`, or a
  `list_ordered()` that only passes `order_by=`, is a wrapper of the SDK:
  call `list` / `get` / `paginate` with the filters at the call site.

### 5. Controller (`src/controllers/products/`)

```python
# src/controllers/products/product.py
from tempest_fastapi_sdk import BaseController

from src.schemas import ProductCreateSchema, ProductResponseSchema
from src.services import ProductService


class ProductController(BaseController[ProductService, ProductResponseSchema]):
    """Coordination layer for products."""

    async def create(self, data: ProductCreateSchema) -> ProductResponseSchema:
        """Create a product.

        Args:
            data (ProductCreateSchema): The creation payload.

        Returns:
            ProductResponseSchema: The created product.
        """
        return await self.service.create(data)
```

```python
# src/controllers/products/__init__.py
from .product import ProductController as ProductController

__all__: list[str] = ["ProductController"]
```

Keep the controller **even when every method is a pass-through** — it is
the seam where cross-service coordination lands later, and its presence
keeps the import graph identical across services. A controller receives
services, never the session and never another controller.

### 6. Composition and provider

Assemble the graph once, in the composition root — **add** the builder
next to `build_service_info_controller`:

```python
# src/scripts/composition.py
from sqlalchemy.ext.asyncio import AsyncSession

from src.controllers import ProductController
from src.db.repositories import ProductRepository
from src.services import ProductService


def build_product_controller(session: AsyncSession) -> ProductController:
    """Assemble the product controller over one session.

    Args:
        session (AsyncSession): The session the repository reads through.

    Returns:
        ProductController: The controller, fully wired.
    """
    return ProductController(ProductService(ProductRepository(session)))
```

Then the request provider, next to `get_service_info_controller`:

```python
# src/api/dependencies/controllers.py
from fastapi import Depends
from sqlalchemy.ext.asyncio import AsyncSession

from src.api.dependencies.resources import get_session
from src.controllers import ProductController
from src.scripts import build_product_controller


def get_product_controller(
    session: AsyncSession = Depends(get_session),
) -> ProductController:
    """Build the product controller for one request.

    Args:
        session (AsyncSession): The request-scoped session.

    Returns:
        ProductController: A controller wired to that session.
    """
    return build_product_controller(session)
```

Every input of a provider is another `Depends` — `get_session`,
`get_settings` (from `src.core`, as `get_service_info_controller` uses
it), and `get_mailer` / `get_storage` from
`src/api/dependencies/resources.py` when the `[email]` / `[minio]` extras
are pinned — never a module-level `settings` or singleton read in the
body. That is what lets a test swap any of them with
`app.dependency_overrides[get_settings] = lambda: Settings(...)` without
touching the router, the controller or the service. Re-export the
builder from `src/scripts/__init__.py` and the provider from
`src/api/dependencies/__init__.py`.

### 7. Router (`src/api/routers/`)

One router per module, declaring only its own prefix:

```python
# src/api/routers/product.py
from fastapi import APIRouter, Depends, status

from src.api.dependencies import get_product_controller
from src.controllers import ProductController
from src.schemas import ProductCreateSchema, ProductResponseSchema

router: APIRouter = APIRouter(prefix="/products", tags=["products"])


@router.post("", status_code=status.HTTP_201_CREATED)
async def create_product(
    data: ProductCreateSchema,
    controller: ProductController = Depends(get_product_controller),
) -> ProductResponseSchema:
    """Create a product.

    Args:
        data (ProductCreateSchema): The creation payload.
        controller (ProductController): Injected controller.

    Returns:
        ProductResponseSchema: The created product.
    """
    return await controller.create(data)
```

Mount it in `build_api_router`, the only place `/api` is written:

```python
# src/api/routers/api.py
from fastapi import APIRouter

from .product import router as product_router
from .service_info import router as service_info_router

API_PREFIX: str = "/api"
"""Prefix every business route lives under."""


def build_api_router() -> APIRouter:
    """Assemble the router ``create_app`` mounts.

    Returns:
        APIRouter: ``/api`` with every domain router included.
    """
    api: APIRouter = APIRouter(prefix=API_PREFIX)
    api.include_router(service_info_router)
    api.include_router(product_router)
    return api


__all__: list[str] = ["API_PREFIX", "build_api_router"]
```

### 8. Tests (`tests/`)

Every test says, in its docstring, the situation it builds and what it
proves, and checks something:

```python
# tests/test_products.py
from fastapi.testclient import TestClient

from src.api.app import create_app


def test_a_duplicate_name_is_refused() -> None:
    """Cenário: dois produtos criados com o mesmo nome.

    Esperado: o segundo responde ``409`` com ``PRODUCT_NAME_TAKEN``.
    """
    with TestClient(create_app()) as client:
        payload = {"name": "Camisa", "price_cents": 4990}
        assert client.post("/api/products", json=payload).status_code == 201
        duplicate = client.post("/api/products", json=payload)
    assert duplicate.status_code == 409
    assert duplicate.json()["code"] == "PRODUCT_NAME_TAKEN"
```

Name the exception a test expects (`pytest.raises(ProductNotFoundError)`,
never `Exception`), and keep `parametrize` values literal: a `uuid4()`
turned into text in an id makes each `pytest -n` worker collect different
tests.

## What `tempest check` enforces

`tempest check` runs `tempest lint-layers` between `mypy` and `pytest`;
run it alone while you work. Each violation prints as
`path:line: CODE message`, and the message says what to change. An
**error** fails the gate; a **warning** prints `(warning)` and fails only
with `--strict` or `layers_strict = true` under `[tool.tempest]`.

| Code | Severity | Reports |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `SVC001` | error | a public class in `services/` without `BaseService` |
| `SVC002` | error | a second public class or a module-level function in `services/` |
| `SVC003` | error | a service `__init__` that only stores the repository |
| `REPO001` | error | a repository method that only forwards to the base |
| `ENUM001` | error | a `BaseStrEnum` subclass not named `*Enum` |
| `RTR001` | error | a `*Controller(...)` / `*Service(...)` built inside `routers/` |
| `RTR002` | error | a route that returns an ORM model |
| `LAY001` | warning | a second class or a module-level function in `controllers/`, `repositories/` or `models/` |
| `LAY002` | warning | a module at the root of a layer, outside a context package |
| `REPO002` | warning | a repository importing another repository |
| `DI001` | warning | `AsyncSession` in a service or controller `__init__` |
| `DI002` | warning | a service taking a `*Service`, or a controller taking a `*Controller` |
| `DI003` | warning | a `from_session` anywhere |
| `STR001` | warning | a function defined inside another function (source and tests) |
| `STR002` | warning | a module-level `_name` function (source and tests) |
| `IMP001` | warning | an absolute import of a neighbour in `__init__.py` |
| `IMP002` | warning | importing a submodule for a name a package above re-exports |
| `RTR003` | warning | more than one `APIRouter` in a routers module |
| `RTR004` | warning | a `/api` prefix outside `build_api_router` |
| `TST001` | warning | a test docstring without `Cenário:` and `Esperado:` |
| `TST002` | warning | a test that checks nothing |
| `TST003` | warning | `pytest.raises(Exception)` |
| `TST004` | warning | a `parametrize` id holding a value drawn at import |

Migrate a rule at a time with `tempest lint-layers --rule <CODE>`.

**An exception you keep on purpose is declared, with its reason**, in
`tests/architecture_exceptions.py` — reviewed in the diff like code:

```python
ARCHITECTURE_EXCEPTIONS: dict[str, str] = {
    "DI002 src/services/wallet/settlement.py::SettlementService.wallet_service": (
        "the settlement and the wallet credit land in the same transaction"
    ),
}
```

The key is `"<CODE> <path>"` or `"<CODE> <path>::<subject>"`; an entry
that no longer matches anything is reported (`EXC002`), so an exception
does not outlive the code it excused. Two markers still work on their
line: `# tempest: no-base-service` (`SVC001`) and
`# tempest: router-builds` (`RTR001`).

## Guards: authentication in the route, invariants with `@requires`

Two places check permissions, and they answer different questions:

- **"Who is calling?"** — `make_token_dependency`,
  `make_jwt_user_dependency`, `make_role_dependency`,
  `make_permission_dependency`. A route dependency, resolved before the
  handler; the router passes the user it resolved on to the controller.
- **"Does this user, already loaded, pass this invariant?"** —
  `@requires(guard, ...)` with `require_authenticated`, `require_active`,
  `require_admin`, `make_flag_guard`, `make_value_guard` or a guard of your own. It goes on
  the **controller method** of the use case, so every caller — HTTP
  route, queue consumer, CLI command — goes through the same check.

```python
from uuid import UUID

from tempest_fastapi_sdk import BaseUserModel, requires
from tempest_fastapi_sdk.auth import require_admin


class ProductAdminActions:
    """The use case shape: the guard sits on the method, not the route."""

    @requires(require_admin)
    async def remove(self, product_id: UUID, user: BaseUserModel) -> None:
        """Delete a product; administrators only.

        Args:
            product_id (UUID): The product to delete.
            user (BaseUserModel): The caller, already authenticated.
        """
```

The guard finds the user by its annotation (a `BaseUserModel` subclass)
and raises `ForbiddenException`, which the registered handlers turn into
the `403` envelope. `tempest permissions` checks every `@requires` against
its contract statically (a guard list left empty, no parameter annotated
with a user model, a guard that returns `bool` instead of raising), so
`tempest permissions` belongs in CI next to `tempest check`.

## Errors: raise, never build a response

The SDK's exception hierarchy is registered on the app by
`register_exception_handlers(app)`. Raise a domain exception and the
envelope, the status code and the OpenAPI entry come for free.

```python
# src/core/exceptions.py
from tempest_fastapi_sdk import ConflictException, NotFoundException


class ProductNotFoundError(NotFoundException):
    """Raised when a product id matches no stored record."""

    code = "PRODUCT_NOT_FOUND"


class ProductNameTakenError(ConflictException):
    """Raised when a product name is already registered."""

    code = "PRODUCT_NAME_TAKEN"
```

- **Always declare `code` in the class body.** Without it the exception
  inherits the SDK's generic identifier (`"NOT_FOUND"`, `"CONFLICT"`),
  which no client can tell apart from any other failure with the same
  status — the SDK raises `InheritedErrorCodeWarning` when you forget.
- **Never** raise `HTTPException` from a service or controller.
- **Never** return `{"error": ...}` by hand.
- A single-resource lookup that misses raises the `*NotFoundError`.
  A collection that matches nothing returns `[]` — an empty list is a
  successful result, not a 404. Do not define plural `*sNotFoundError`.

## Pagination

Use the SDK envelope; do not invent a page shape.

```python
from fastapi import APIRouter, Depends
from tempest_fastapi_sdk import BasePaginationFilterSchema, BasePaginationSchema

from src.api.dependencies import get_product_controller
from src.controllers import ProductController
from src.schemas import ProductResponseSchema

router: APIRouter = APIRouter(prefix="/products", tags=["products"])


@router.get("")
async def list_products(
    filters: BasePaginationFilterSchema = Depends(),
    controller: ProductController = Depends(get_product_controller),
) -> BasePaginationSchema[ProductResponseSchema]:
    """List products, paginated.

    Args:
        filters (BasePaginationFilterSchema): Page, size and ordering.
        controller (ProductController): Injected controller.

    Returns:
        BasePaginationSchema[ProductResponseSchema]: One page of products.
    """
    result = await controller.paginate(
        filters=filters.get_conditions(),
        order_by=filters.order_by,
        page=filters.page,
        page_size=filters.page_size,
        ascending=filters.ascending,
    )
    return BasePaginationSchema[ProductResponseSchema](**result)
```

`controller.paginate` returns the plain mapping
`{items, total, page, page_size, pages}` that the envelope accepts
directly. Subclass `BasePaginationFilterSchema` to add domain filters —
`get_conditions()` then carries them and the pagination keys stay
separate.

## The `ui` layer (only with the `[ssr]` extra)

If this project serves HTML, it has `src/ui/` — a layer at the same level
as `controllers` and `services`, answering only *what does this look
like*.

- **A page never performs I/O.** No `await` on a repository, service or
  HTTP client inside `body()`. The router loads through the controller
  and passes the data into the page's typed fields.
- **One class per screen** in `ui/pages/`, each subclassing `BasePage`
  from `ui/layout/`, which owns `shell()`.
- **Forms come from schemas** — never hand-written `<input>` markup:

  ```python
  from fastapi import Request
  from fastapi.responses import RedirectResponse, Response
  from tempest_fastapi_sdk.ssr import html_response
  from tempest_fastapi_sdk.ui.forms import form_for, parse_form

  from src.schemas import ProductCreateSchema
  from src.ui import CSS_URL


  async def create_product_form(request: Request) -> Response:
      """Validate the submitted product form."""
      result = await parse_form(ProductCreateSchema, request)
      if not result.ok:
          return html_response(
              form_for(
                  ProductCreateSchema,
                  action="/products/new",
                  values=result.values,
                  errors=result.errors,
              ),
              title="Novo produto",
              status_code=422,
              stylesheets=[CSS_URL],
          )
      return RedirectResponse("/products", status_code=303)
  ```

- **Styling is the typed sheet in `src/ui/styles.py`**, served by
  `make_css_router(STYLESHEET, path=CSS_PATH)`; pages link `CSS_URL`
  (`STYLESHEET.url(CSS_PATH)`, the content-versioned URL), never the bare
  path, so a deploy is never new HTML on top of a cached old sheet.
  Reference design tokens (`THEME.color("surface")`, `THEME.space("md")`)
  instead of hard-coding colours, and add rules to `_OWN_RULES` rather
  than writing a `.css` file.
- **Tables are `DataTable`, even with links or forms per row**: a
  `TableColumn("name", render=...)` cell returns widgets, `align="right"`
  lines numbers up, and `class_name=` targets a column from the sheet.
- **Use `Stack` for semantic markup** (`<select>`, `<table>`, `<ul>`) and
  `Column`/`Row` only where a flex box is wanted.
- Reuse the shipped components: `Card`, `Alert`, `DataTable`,
  `Pagination`, `EmptyState`, `NavBar`, `Shell`, `Grid`.
- HTML routes live in `api/routers/web.py` with
  `include_in_schema=False`, so they never pollute the OpenAPI schema.

@@if tasks@@
## Background tasks (`src/tasks/`)

A task module is **registration only**: the decorator, a name and a
schedule from `src/tasks/names.py`, and a body that calls the work in
`src/scripts/`. The API never imports a task module — it enqueues through
`TaskDispatcher` (`src/tasks/dispatch.py`), by name:

```python
from fastapi import Depends

from src.tasks import TaskDispatcher, get_task_dispatcher


async def run_now(dispatcher: TaskDispatcher = Depends(get_task_dispatcher)) -> None:
    """Ask the worker to run the example task."""
    await dispatcher.example("payload")
```

- A name (`*_TASK_NAME`) and a schedule (`*_SCHEDULE`, built with
  `every_n_minutes` / `daily` / `weekly`) are constants in `names.py`,
  which imports nothing from the project. **A task keeps its name for
  life**: messages already queued under an old name are dropped.
- A new task module is imported by `src/tasks/worker.py`, or it never
  registers. The worker runs `taskiq worker src.tasks.worker:broker`; the
  scheduler, `taskiq scheduler src.tasks.worker:scheduler`.
- The retry policy a task is registered with still applies to a call sent
  by name — the worker restores the task's labels.

@@endif@@
## Do not reimplement these

| You are about to write | Use instead |
| --- | --- |
| CRUD queries, filters, pagination | `BaseRepository` |
| A page envelope for a listing | `BasePaginationSchema` / `BasePaginationFilterSchema` |
| An error response body | `AppException` subclasses + `register_exception_handlers` |
| Password hashing, JWT encode/decode | `PasswordUtils`, `JWTUtils` |
| A signup/login/reset flow | `UserAuthService` + `make_auth_router` |
| A health endpoint | `make_health_router` |
| CORS, request ids, rate limiting | `apply_cors`, `RequestIDMiddleware`, `RateLimitMiddleware` |
| Redis caching, cache invalidation | `AsyncRedisManager`, `@cached`, `CacheInvalidator` |
| RabbitMQ publish/subscribe | `MessageBroker` (`[queue]`) |
| Background jobs and cron | `TaskQueue` (`[tasks]`) |
| File upload/download handling | `UploadUtils`, `DownloadUtils`, `FileStoreUtils` |
| Sending email | `EmailUtils` |
| A CRUD admin UI | `AdminSite` + `AdminModel` (`[admin]`) |
| HTML pages, forms, CSS | `tempest_fastapi_sdk.ui` (`[ssr]`) |
| CPF/CNPJ/CEP/phone validation | the SDK's Brazilian field types |
| Alembic boilerplate | `AlembicHelper` and `tempest db` |

If the SDK covers 90% of what you need, extend it in this project rather
than forking the concept — and open an issue upstream for the missing
10%.

## Code conventions

- **Double quotes** for every string.
- **Full type annotations** on every function, method, parameter and
  module-level variable — including `-> None`.
- **Google-style docstrings in English** on every module, class, function
  and method: description, `Args`, `Returns`, `Raises`. Test docstrings
  carry `Cenário:` and `Esperado:`.
- **No explanatory inline comments.** The *why* belongs in the docstring.
  Only machine-directed markers (`# noqa`, `# type: ignore`) stay inline.
- **No function defined inside another**, and no module-level `_helper`.
  A callback that keeps state is a class with `__call__`; one that binds
  an argument is `functools.partial` over a module-level function.
- **`async` by default** for anything doing I/O. SQLAlchemy 2.0 style
  (`select()`), never `session.query()`.
- **Re-export from every `__init__.py`** using a relative
  `from .x import Y as Y` plus an explicit `__all__: list[str]`, and
  import from the package level (`from src.schemas import
  ProductCreateSchema`), never from the submodule.
- Collections default to empty: `Field(default_factory=list)` in
  schemas, `Query(default_factory=list)` for repeated query parameters.
- Routers return schemas, never ORM models.

## Commands

```bash
uv sync                     # install dependencies
cp .env.example .env        # first run only
uv run python main.py       # start the service on __HOST__:__PORT__

tempest check               # lint + format check + mypy + layer rules + pytest
tempest lint-layers         # only the layer rules, errors and warnings
tempest lint-layers --rule IMP002   # one rule, while migrating
tempest lint-schemas        # schema fields without title/description/examples
tempest lint                # ruff check
tempest fmt                 # ruff format
tempest type                # mypy
tempest test                # pytest

tempest db revision -m "add products"   # autogenerate a migration
tempest db upgrade                      # apply migrations (startup does it too)
tempest generate --src                  # add layers for newly pinned extras
tempest generate --docker               # regenerate docker-compose.yaml
```

**Run `tempest check` before declaring any task finished.** A change that
does not pass it is not done.

## Definition of done

- [ ] The change lives in the layer that owns it, in its context package.
- [ ] Every new function, method and class is typed and documented.
- [ ] New public symbols are re-exported from their `__init__.py` and
      listed in `__all__`.
- [ ] Domain errors raise SDK exceptions; collections return `[]`.
- [ ] Tests cover the new behaviour (`pytest` + `pytest-asyncio`,
      in-memory SQLite, external services mocked), each with
      `Cenário:` / `Esperado:`.
- [ ] `tempest check` passes, with no new warning.
- [ ] A schema, endpoint or env var changed? `README.md` and
      `.env.example` were updated in the same commit.
